.TH MACH 1 "October 2026" "Mach 0.10.0" "Mach Manual"
.SH NAME
mach \- supersonic, lightweight Linux virtualization and command runner for macOS Apple Silicon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mach
.br
.B mach
[\fB\-m\fR \fImachine\fR] [\fB\-u\fR \fIuser\fR] [\fB\-w\fR \fIdir\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs...\fR]
.br
.B mach
\fIsubcommand\fR [\fIargs...\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mach
is a lightweight, zero-overhead Linux virtualization and container engine designed specifically for Apple Silicon Macs. Written in Rust and powered by Apple's native \fBVirtualization.framework\fR, Mach starts full Linux environments in under one second with less than 15 MB host memory overhead.

\fBmach\fR can be used in three distinct ways:

.TP
.B 1. Interactive Linux Shell
Running \fBmach\fR with no arguments opens an interactive login shell inside the default Linux machine.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mach
root@debian:~# uname -a
Linux debian 6.1.0-53-cloud-arm64 #1 SMP aarch64
.fi
.RE

.TP
.B 2. Transparent Command Execution
Prefix any command with \fBmach\fR to execute it transparently inside the guest Linux environment, streaming output, exit codes, and signals directly back to your macOS terminal.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mach uname -a
$ mach cat /etc/os-release
$ mach -m debian -u root apt update
.fi
.RE

.TP
.B 3. Machine & Subsystem Management
Any \fBmachctl\fR administration subcommand can be invoked directly through \fBmach\fR.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mach start debian
$ mach top
$ mach docker setup
$ mach k8s enable
.fi
.RE

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-m " " machine ", " \-\-machine " " machine
Specify the target Linux virtual machine (defaults to the configured default machine, or the first running machine).
.TP
.BI \-u " " user ", " \-\-user " " user
Specify the guest username to run the command as (e.g., \fIroot\fR, \fIdebian\fR, \fIubuntu\fR).
.TP
.BI \-w " " dir ", " \-\-workdir " " dir
Set the working directory inside the guest VM before executing the command.
.TP
.B \-h\fR, \fB\-\-help
Print summary help and options.
.TP
.B \-V\fR, \fB\-\-version
Print the Mach version number.

.SH ENVIRONMENT & FILE SHARING
.TP
.B /Users Parity
The entire macOS \fB/Users\fR hierarchy is shared directly into the Linux guest via Apple VirtioFS at \fB/Users\fR, providing 1:1 path parity and sub-millisecond I/O latency.
.TP
.B Dynamic Port Forwarding
Container ports exposed inside the guest VM are automatically bound on macOS localhost (\fB127.0.0.1\fR) in user space without requiring root privileges.
.TP
.B Local DNS Routing
All guest machines and Docker containers are accessible through the local \fB*.mach.local\fR domain via the built-in async DNS responder.
.TP
.B Rosetta 2 Translation
Intel \fBx86_64\fR binaries and Linux containers run seamlessly on Apple Silicon via hardware-accelerated Rosetta 2 for Linux (\fBdocker run --platform linux/amd64\fR).

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B Open default Linux shell
mach
.TP
.B Run a quick Linux command from macOS
mach uname -m
.TP
.B Execute as root in a specific machine
mach -m fedora -u root dnf install -y htop
.TP
.B Monitor resources in real-time
mach top
.TP
.B Configure Docker CLI context
mach docker setup

